\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Trade-offs at $N=60$ (Model B). (a) Raising the value of time $\lambda _d$ shortens trips much faster than it lowers satisfaction, until $\lambda _d=0.32$. (b) Distribution of user value $U_i$, lower tail only: the fairness floor lifts the worst-off users from about $-0.5$ to about $4$ for a 1--3\% drop in total value. (c) Share of tables that drop below four people in 5{,}000 simulated evenings, with the average table size above each bar.}}{6}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:trade}{{3}{6}{Trade-offs at $N=60$ (Model B). (a) Raising the value of time $\lambda _d$ shortens trips much faster than it lowers satisfaction, until $\lambda _d=0.32$. (b) Distribution of user value $U_i$, lower tail only: the fairness floor lifts the worst-off users from about $-0.5$ to about $4$ for a 1--3\% drop in total value. (c) Share of tables that drop below four people in 5{,}000 simulated evenings, with the average table size above each bar}{figure.caption.4}{}}
